@database "mcp_client"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: mcp_client.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "MCP client"
@{h1}MCP client

Other programs offer tools over the Model Context Protocol (MCP): a
documentation search, a Blender bridge, a company's own service. This
plugin connects ScarabHive to such external MCP servers and hands their
tools to the agents, next to the tools of the local plugins. A server is
either started as a local program (stdio) or dialled over HTTP. You
declare the servers in @{tt}config/mcp_servers.yaml@{utt}; an agent uses a
server's tools only when its allowlist names them. The plugin also has
four small tools, and matching chat commands, to see and steer the
connections.

An external server is foreign code: a stdio server runs with the
server's rights, and whatever it says about its tools and in its answers
reaches the model. Read @{" Security and limits " link security} before
you add one.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Chat commands  " link commands}
  @{"  Security and limits  " link security}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  External tools  " link external}
  @{"  The management tools  " link tools}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node commands "Chat commands"
@{h1}Chat commands

Four commands at the chat prompt run the plugin's management tools
through the agent's own dispatch. They work only for an agent whose
allowlist grants @{tt}mcp_client/*@{utt}.

@{table}
Command | Does
/mcp | the enabled servers with connection state, transport and protocol version
/mcp-connect <server> | connect a server, or reconnect one whose connection dropped
/mcp-disconnect <server> | close a server's connection
/mcp-tools [server] | the tools the connected servers offer, blocked ones marked
@{body}

@{tt}/mcp-connect@{utt} on a server that is still connected does nothing
and reports success. To restart a live server -- a stdio program that
hangs, a server whose tools changed -- disconnect it first, then connect.

The connections belong to the process: the API server holds one set for
every user and every agent, and a disconnect there is a disconnect for
everyone (see @{" Security and limits " link security}).

On the command line, @{tt}agent-cli mcp list@{utt}, @{tt}status@{utt},
@{tt}tools <server>@{utt} and @{tt}test <server>@{utt} inspect the same
configuration from a process of their own.
@endnode

@node external "External tools"
@{h1}External tools

@{h2}Names and allowlists

An external tool is called @{tt}<server>.<tool>@{utt}, with a dot:
@{tt}everything.get-sum@{utt}, @{tt}blender.execute_blender_code@{utt}. The
dot is what the runtime uses to send a call out to a server instead of to
a local plugin, and what the permission patterns match on. An agent opts
in with the dot form:

@{code yaml}
tools:
  allowed:
    - "+everything.*"               # every tool of the server 'everything'
    - "+blender.get_scene_info"     # one tool by name
@{body}

@{tt}everything/*@{utt} or a bare @{tt}everything@{utt} do NOT admit
dotted tools, and @{tt}mcp_client/*@{utt} grants only the four
management tools, no external tool. An agent's own
@{tt}tools.blocked@{utt} patterns apply to external tools as well.

Only connected servers contribute tools. When a server failed to
connect, its tools were never discovered and every pattern for it
matches nothing -- look for "External MCP servers: N connected, M
failed" in the log before you suspect the pattern.

@{h2}Blocked tools

A server's @{tt}tools.blocked@{utt} list in @{tt}mcp_servers.yaml@{utt}
refuses those tools by exact name. They are not offered to any model,
whatever an agent's allowlist says; @{tt}/mcp-tools@{utt} and the
management tool still show them, marked @{tt}blocked: true@{utt}. A call
that names one anyway is refused with "Tool '<tool>' is blocked on MCP
server '<server>'". The server's @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} list is not
read.

@{h2}What a call returns

The arguments go to the server as the model wrote them; keys beginning
with @{tt}_@{utt} (the runtime's own) are removed first. The answer:

@{table}
The server sends | The model gets
an error result (isError) | an error with the server's text blocks
image or audio blocks | the files, written to disk and shown to the model as media; the text blocks as message
text blocks | all of them in order, a blank line apart, as one string; resource blocks next to them are dropped
no text block | the structured content, or else the whole result as JSON
@{body}

An answer longer than @{tt}max_result_chars@{utt} (50000 characters) is
cut there and ends with @{tt}…[<full length> chars]@{utt}; for media
answers the message is cut. A structured answer is measured as JSON, and
when it is too long it goes on cut, as text. A server's error text is cut
the same way.

Image and audio blocks are written to
@{tt}media/external_mcp/<server>/@{utt} in the data directory, named after
the tool (characters other than letters, digits, @{tt}_ . -@{utt} become
@{tt}_@{utt}), the time and a random part, so no file replaces another. Nothing cleans that directory up. Video has no
MCP content block of its own; a server that sends video as a resource is
not unpacked.

@{h2}Timeouts

@{table}
What | Limit
the handshake of a new connection | the server's timeout, but at least 60 seconds; then "did not complete the handshake"
one call or tool listing | the server's timeout; then "did not answer within Ns", and the call is abandoned
closing a connection | the server's timeout, then the connection is cancelled
@{body}

The server's timeout is its own @{tt}timeout@{utt}, else
@{tt}external_servers.connection.timeout@{utt} (5 seconds in the shipped
file). Slow tools need their own: the Blender entry sets 300, because a
render takes minutes. The handshake gets at least a minute whatever the
timeout, because a stdio program has to start first -- through
@{tt}npx@{utt} it downloads its package on the first start. If that takes
longer, install the package (@{tt}everything_local@{utt} shows how).

Calls to one server run side by side: a slow call does not hold up the
others. When a call times out, the waiting call is cancelled; the server
may still be working on it.

@{h2}Starting, dropping, reconnecting

When the plugin starts it connects every enabled server, all at the
same time. A server that fails is logged and skipped; it does not stop
the others or the start. Servers that never answer hold the start up
once for the longest handshake limit among them (a stdio program about
two seconds more to be ended), not once each. A disconnect that comes
while a server is still shaking hands wins: the new connection is
closed again.

When a connection drops -- a stdio program exits, a remote server goes
away -- nothing reconnects it by itself. Its calls fail with "External MCP
server '<server>' is not connected" until someone reconnects it with
@{tt}/mcp-connect@{utt} or the management tool, or the process restarts.
The agents' tool list may keep showing the dropped server's tools until
the next connect or disconnect.
@endnode

@node tools "The management tools"
@{h1}The management tools

Four tools, named after the plugin instance (@{tt}mcp_client_...@{utt} in
the default setup). They need @{tt}mcp_client/*@{utt} in the allowlist.

@{table}
Tool | Parameters | Answers
mcp_client_list_servers | none | servers (name, description, url, transport, enabled, connected, server_info, protocol_version), connected (names), total
mcp_client_connect | server (required) | success, server, server_info (name, version), protocol_version
mcp_client_disconnect | server (required) | success, server, message: "disconnected" or "was not connected"
mcp_client_tools | server (optional), force_refresh | servers: name to a list of name, description, input_schema, blocked; total
@{body}

@{bullet}@{tt}list_servers@{utt} shows the enabled servers only; a server
with @{tt}enabled: false@{utt} is not there.
@{bullet}@{tt}connect@{utt} answers @{tt}success: false@{utt} with an
@{tt}error@{utt} naming the cause: "External MCP server '<server>' is not
configured or not enabled", "Could not connect to MCP server '<server>':
<cause>", "MCP server '<server>' did not complete the handshake within
Ns", "... has unknown transport ...", "... has no url configured",
"... uses transport 'stdio' but has no 'command' configured".
@{bullet}@{tt}disconnect@{utt} with a server that was not connected
answers @{tt}success: false@{utt}, "was not connected" -- an answer, not
an error.
@{bullet}@{tt}tools@{utt} with the name of a server that is not
connected answers an empty list for it. The tool list is kept for
@{tt}external_servers.cache.tool_list_ttl@{utt} seconds, and dropped on
every connect and disconnect; @{tt}force_refresh: true@{utt} asks the
servers again. A server that does not answer the listing keeps its last
known tools.
@{bullet}Without @{tt}server@{utt}, connect and disconnect answer "Parameter
'server' is required".

@{code json}
{"server": "everything"}
{"server": "everything", "force_refresh": true}
@{body}
@endnode

@node security "Security and limits"
@{h1}Security and limits

@{h2}A stdio server is a program you run

A stdio entry is started as a child process of the API server (or of
@{tt}agent-cli@{utt}), with its rights, in the folder the server was
started in. Its standard error goes to the server's own. It does not get
the server's environment: only a fixed set of variables
(@{tt}PATH@{utt}, @{tt}PATHEXT@{utt}, @{tt}SYSTEMROOT@{utt},
@{tt}TEMP@{utt}, @{tt}APPDATA@{utt}, @{tt}USERPROFILE@{utt} and a few more
on Windows; @{tt}HOME@{utt}, @{tt}PATH@{utt}, @{tt}SHELL@{utt},
@{tt}USER@{utt} and a few more elsewhere) plus what the entry's
@{tt}env@{utt} sets. API keys in the server's environment do not reach
it unless you pass them there. @{tt}${VAR}@{utt} in the file is replaced
from the environment when the configuration is read.

Pin the version of what you start: @{tt}npx -y <package>@{utt} without a
version fetches whatever is newest on every start.

@{h2}HTTP servers

@{tt}auth@{utt} adds a header: @{tt}bearer@{utt} (Authorization: Bearer),
@{tt}api_key@{utt} (the key in @{tt}api_key_header@{utt}, default
Authorization) or @{tt}basic@{utt}. HTTPS certificates are checked when
@{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt} is true, as the shipped
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} sets it. A configuration without that key
checks NO certificates -- the setting's default is false. Redirects are
followed.

@{h2}What a server can put in front of the model

Everything a server says reaches the model:

@{bullet}Its tool descriptions and parameter schemas go into the tool
definitions the model reads on every call. The plugin marks them only
with @{tt}[<server>]@{utt} in front and cuts a description at
@{tt}max_description_chars@{utt} (1024); the parameter schemas pass
whole. They reach the model once an agent's allowlist admits the tool --
with @{tt}<server>.*@{utt} that is every tool the server announces, now
or after its next update.
@{bullet}Its answers come back as ordinary tool results, cut at
@{tt}max_result_chars@{utt} (50000). The runtime removes invisible
characters (zero-width and bidi marks, tag characters) from them; it does
not mark them as foreign.

An answer can carry instructions aimed at the model. Admit only the
tools an agent needs, by name, from servers you trust.

@{h2}Several users

One process, one set of connections. In the API server every user's
agents use the same connections with the same credentials: whoever's
agent may call @{tt}<server>.*@{utt} acts as the account configured in
@{tt}auth@{utt}. A disconnect through the management tools or
@{tt}/mcp-disconnect@{utt} closes the server for everyone. Images and
audio from all users land in the same directory. Grant
@{tt}mcp_client/*@{utt} only to agents trusted with that.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The four management tools are described by the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. An external tool reaches the model as
@{tt}<server>_<tool>@{utt} -- the dot becomes @{tt}_@{utt}, a slash
@{tt}__@{utt}, and characters outside letters, digits, @{tt}_@{utt} and
@{tt}-@{utt} are removed -- with the description @{tt}[<server>]
<the server's description>@{utt} and the server's input schema as the
parameters.

@{h2}Errors

A failed external call reaches the model as
@{tt}{"error": "Tool invocation failed: <message>"}@{utt}, with messages
such as:

@{bullet}"External MCP server 'everything' is not connected"
@{bullet}"Tool 'x' is blocked on MCP server 'everything'"
@{bullet}"MCP server 'everything' did not answer within 5.0s"
@{bullet}"Tool call failed: <the server's error text>"
@{bullet}"MCP server 'everything' disconnected: <cause>"

@{h2}The status line

An external call shows "Starting tool call: <tool>", then "Tool call
completed: <tool>" or "Tool call failed: <tool>". The management tools
end with "2 server(s) configured, 1 connected", "Connected everything
(protocol 2025-06-18)", "everything: disconnected", "13 tool(s) on
everything" or "everything: not connected".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Results are appended like any tool result; nothing earlier changes. The
tool definitions change when a server connects, drops or disconnects,
or updates its tools -- then the tools section of the next call differs
and the provider's prompt cache starts again from there.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Two external tools whose names differ only in characters that
are removed or replaced (@{tt}a.b_c@{utt} and @{tt}a_b.c@{utt}) get the
same name for the model.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration. Leave it on even when no
external server is: the runtime asks it for external tools, and without
it an outage looks like "nothing configured".

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    mcp_client:
      type: mcp_client
      enabled: true
@{body}

It needs the @{tt}mcp@{utt} package (1.14.1 or newer). The management tools
need @{tt}"+mcp_client/*"@{utt} in an agent's allowlist; external tools
need their dotted names, see @{" External tools " link external}.

@{h2}Declaring servers

Servers are declared in @{tt}config/mcp_servers.yaml@{utt}, under
@{tt}external_servers.remote_servers@{utt}. Declare them in that file: a
later configuration file that declares @{tt}remote_servers@{utt} replaces
the whole list instead of adding to it.

@{code yaml}
external_servers:
  connection:
    timeout: 5.0
  cache:
    tool_list_ttl: 3600.0
  remote_servers:
    docs:                          # a remote server
      enabled: true
      transport: streaming
      url: "https://mcp.example.org/mcp"
      auth:
        type: bearer
        bearer_token: "${DOCS_MCP_TOKEN}"
      tools:
        blocked: [delete_page]
    blender:                       # a local program over stdio
      enabled: false
      transport: stdio
      timeout: 300
      command: external/mcp/blender_mcp/.venv/Scripts/blender-mcp.exe
      env:
        BLENDER_MCP_DISABLE_TELEMETRY: "true"
@{body}

@{h2}Server entry

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
enabled | false | only enabled servers are connected, listed and callable
transport | streaming | streaming, streamable_http, streamable-http, http, smithery: streamable HTTP; sse, http_sse, http+sse: HTTP with SSE; stdio, local: start a program
url | empty | the endpoint, for the HTTP transports
command, args, env | none | the program, its arguments and extra environment, for stdio
timeout | the connection timeout | seconds for the handshake and for each answer
auth | none | type none, bearer, api_key or basic, with bearer_token, api_key and api_key_header (Authorization), username and password
tools.blocked | none | tool names a call is refused for
description | none | shown in the server listing
@{body}

Read but without effect: @{tt}initialization_options@{utt} (the protocol
has no such field; connecting logs a warning that it is not sent),
@{tt}features@{utt}, @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt}, @{tt}auth.max_retries@{utt}
and @{tt}auth.retry_delay@{utt}.

@{h2}Plugin settings

In the plugin's entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}; a value that is
not a positive whole number means the default.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
max_result_chars | 50000 | characters of an external answer the model gets; the rest is cut
max_description_chars | 1024 | characters of a foreign tool description; it is sent with every request of every agent that allows the tool
@{body}

@{h2}Shared settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
connection.timeout | 5 | seconds, for servers without their own timeout
connection.parallel_connect | true | not read: servers always connect at the same time
cache.tool_list_ttl | 30 (shipped file: 3600) | seconds the plugin keeps the tool lists
cache.enabled, cache.max_size | true, none | the runtime's catalogue of all tools, which is kept until a connect, disconnect or configuration change
network.ssl_verify | false (shipped config: true) | check HTTPS certificates
@{body}

Image and audio results are written to @{tt}media/external_mcp/@{utt}
in the data directory.
@endnode
